## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design in-place sorting algorithms, understand stability, and reason about trade-offs between time and memory in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts

- In-place merging
- Stability
- Time/space complexity
- Rotation-based merging

## Code Example

```javascript
function inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right) {
  let i = left, j = mid + 1;
  while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
    if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) { i++; continue; }
    const tmp = arr[j];
    for (let k = j; k > i; k--) arr[k] = arr[k - 1];
    arr[i] = tmp;
    i++; mid++; j++;
  }
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left, mid);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);
  inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize rotations to guarantee O(n log n) time in all cases?
- How does memory locality affect performance compared with standard mergesort?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes translating a string problem into substring subproblems and requires a clear bottom-up DP setup.

## Key Concepts
- Substring DP: dp[i][j] for i..j
- Base cases: i >= j -> 0 insertions
- Bottom-up vs memoization: space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minInsertions(s){
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i = n-2; i >= 0; i--){
    for(let j = i+1; j < n; j++){
      if(s[i] === s[j]) dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1];
      else dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
    }
  }
  return n ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct one palindrome achieving the minimum insertions?
- How would you adapt for streaming input or very large strings with limited memory?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem assesses mastery of graph algorithms on DAGs, including topological sorting, feasibility verification under ordered constraints, and shortest-path computation.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort for DAGs
- DAG shortest-path DP (O(|V| + |E|))
- Feasibility checking with ordered mandatory edges
- Path reconstruction via predecessor pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestConstrainedPath(G, S, T, M) {
  const topo = topoSort(G);
  const idx = (v) => topo.indexOf(v);

  const canReach = (from, to) => {
    if (idx(from) >= idx(to)) return false;
    const reachable = new Set([from]);
    for (let i = idx(from); i < idx(to); i++) {
      for (const edge of G.adj[topo[i]]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(to)) reachable.add(edge.to);
      }
    }
    return reachable.has(to);
  };

  const points = [S, ...M.map(e => e[0]), ...M.map(e => e[1]), T];
  for (let i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!canReach(points[i], points[i + 1])) return null;
  }

  const segmentCost = (src, dst) => {
    const dist = {}; dist[src] = 0;
    for (let i = idx(src); i < idx(dst); i++) {
      const u = topo[i];
      for (const edge of G.adj[u]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(dst)) {
          if (dist[u] + edge.w < (dist[edge.to] ?? Infinity)) {
            dist[edge.to] = dist[u] + edge.w;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return dist[dst];
  };

  let total = 0;
  const fullPath = [S];
  for (const [u, v, w] of M) {
    total += w;
    const seg = segmentCost(fullPath[fullPath.length - 1], u);
    if (seg === undefined) return null;
    total += seg;
  }
  const finalSeg = segmentCost(M[M.length - 1][1], T);
  if (finalSeg === undefined) return null;
  return total + finalSeg;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
In practice, data often contains many duplicates. A 3-way partitioned quicksort minimizes comparisons on duplicates and scales with the number of distinct keys.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- Randomized pivot to avoid worst-case
- Complexity: O(N log D) expected when D distinct keys
- In-place sort with minimal extra space

## Code Example
```javascript
function sort3(a, l=0, r=a.length-1){
  if (l >= r) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random()*(r-l+1))+l];
  let i=l, lt=l, gt=r;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  sort3(a, l, lt-1);
  sort3(a, gt+1, r);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for stable sorting with duplicates?
- What are the trade-offs vs a counting/radix approach on integers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to formalize DP for partitioning into constrained blocks and compare top-down vs bottom-up implementations with proper modulo handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with pruning
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Positive number optimization
- Modulo arithmetic and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function countPartitions(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n) return 1;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];

    let sum = 0, ways = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum > target) break;
      if (sum === target) {
        ways = (ways + dfs(j + 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    return memo[i] = ways;
  }

  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulation sketch
function countPartitionsBottomUp(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n && sum + nums[j] <= target; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum === target) {
        dp[j+1] = (dp[j+1] + dp[i]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Empty input: return 0
- Large target: handle with modulo and pruning
- Single element equal to target: return 1
- No valid partition: return 0

## Correctness Justification
Top-down explores all valid partitions by recursively trying all possible block endings. Memoization avoids recomputation. Bottom-up builds from base case, accumulating ways to reach each position. Both respect positive numbers enabling early termination when sum exceeds target.

## Why This Is Asked
Path auto-complete is a common editor feature; it probes practical data-structure trade-offs beyond textbooks.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node children map
- Prefix search via traversal then DFS for completions
- Memory sharing of prefixes and separator handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
class PathTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insertPath(path) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of path) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, maxResults) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (cur, built) => {
      if (results.length >= maxResults) return;
      if (cur.isEnd) results.push(prefix + built);
      for (const [ch, child] of cur.next) dfs(child, built + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, "");
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory with extremely large path samples (e.g., thousands of common prefixes)?
- How would you support ranking suggestions by frequency or recency without breaking O(length) per insert?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses understanding of stability in sorting and practical merge logic, not just raw sorting.

## Key Concepts

- Stability guarantees in mergesort
- Two-pointer merge and preserving order on ties
- Time complexity O(n log n) and space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSortEventsByTs(arr){
  if (arr.length < 2) return arr;
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length/2);
  const left = stableSortEventsByTs(arr.slice(0, mid));
  const right = stableSortEventsByTs(arr.slice(mid));
  const merged = [];
  let i=0, j=0;
  while (i<left.length && j<right.length){
    if (left[i].ts <= right[j].ts){
      merged.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      merged.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }
  return merged.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test stability with equal timestamps?
- How would you adapt for a streaming pipeline or very large data that doesn't fit in memory?
